 ## Overview
-Neural Scene Graphs (NSG) represent dynamic scenes as a hierarchical directed acyclic graph that decomposes the environment into static background and dynamic object components, enabling novel view synthesis and dynamic scene reconstruction [S1]. Unlike monolithic neural radiance fields, NSG isolates rigidly connected dynamic components into individual nodes with shared class weights and latent codes, allowing for pose-dependent appearance changes while maintaining volumetric consistency [S1].
+Neural Scene Graphs (NSG) represent dynamic scenes as a hierarchical directed acyclic graph (DAG) that separates static background elements from dynamic foreground objects, enabling temporal consistency and accurate modeling of moving components [S1]. Unlike Single Representation Networks (SRNs) that optimize a single static representation for all frames or Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) which suffer from ghosting artifacts due to camera pose changes, NSG reconstructs each object individually using shared weights by class, ensuring distinct appearance and shape modeling for rigidly connected components [S1].
 
 ## How it works
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Inputs:** A set of training images with known camera poses ($T_c^W$) and intrinsics ($K$); 3D tracking data providing object bounding boxes and poses ($p_o$); a scene graph structure $S$.
+*   **Outputs:** Rendered images (pixel colors $\hat{C}$) and learned neural fields for background and dynamic objects.
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
-**Inputs:**
-*   Camera intrinsics $K \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$ and transformation $T_c^W$ (camera to world frame) [S1].
-*   3D tracking data providing transformations $M_{uv}$ for dynamic objects [S1].
-*   Ground truth images for training [S1].
+**Data Structures:**
+The scene is defined as a directed acyclic graph $S = (W, C, F, L, E)$ [S1]:
+*   $C$: Leaf node representing the camera, containing intrinsics $K \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$.
+*   $F$: Leaf nodes representing representation models. $F = F_{\theta_{bckg}} \cup \{F_{\theta_c}\}_{c=1}^{N_{obj}}$, where $F_{\theta_{bckg}}$ is the static background model and $F_{\theta_c}$ are dynamic object models [S1].
+*   $L$: Leaf nodes assigning latent object codes $l_o$ to each representation leaf node. $L = \{l_o\}_{o=1}^{N_{obj}}$ [S1].
+*   $E$: Edges defining the hierarchy and transformations (e.g., camera-to-world, object pose) [S1].
+*   $W$: Implicitly defined by the graph structure and weights [S1].
 
-**Outputs:**
-*   Rendered pixel colors $\hat{C}(r)$ for rays $r$ [S1].
-*   Optimized scene graph $S$ with trained representation networks and latent codes [S1].
