-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Volumetric Consistency**: Object volumetric density must not change based on pose to ensure volumetric consistency; pose is only used for emitted color [S1].
-*   **Bounding Box Scaling**: The scaling matrix $S_o$ must be defined as $\text{diag}(1/s_o)$ where $s_o$ is the bounding box size to enable size-independent learning [S1].
-*   **Plane Sampling**: The static background uses sparse planes instead of a volume, which limits sampling to 2.5D representation [S1].
+**Pitfalls:**
+*   **Volumetric Consistency:** The object volumetric density $\sigma$ must not change based on pose. Pose $p_o$ is only used for emitted color to account for global illumination changes. Including pose in density prediction breaks volumetric consistency [S1].
+*   **Precision:** Fourier encodings are used to handle high-frequency functions; insufficient encoding frequency may lead to blurring [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Neural Scene Graph Representation and Rendering](14688-neural-scene-graph-representation-and-rendering.md)
-A hierarchical scene representation that decomposes scenes into dynamic and static components without additional supervision, where each object is represented as an individual node.
+Renders dynamic scenes by decomposing them into a hierarchical graph of individual object nodes, separating static background from dynamic foreground. It ensures temporal consistency and accurate dynamic modeling, unlike SRNs or NeRFs which suffer from ghosting. It builds on the [Neural Scene Graph](14682-neural-scene-graph.md) structure.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Rendering involves ray-plane intersections for static background and AABB-ray intersections for dynamic objects. Sampling is fixed at $N_s$ and $N_d$ points per intersection [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Depends on the number of dynamic objects and the size of the MLPs for background and object models [S1].
-*   **Performance**: Optimization for a scene takes about a full day or 350k iterations to converge on a single NVIDIA TITAN Xp GPU [S1].
-*   **Strengths**: Hierarchical decomposition allows for dynamic scene reconstruction and novel view synthesis. Shared weights between objects of the same class improve generalization [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: Relies on 3D tracking data for transformations [S1]. Not limited to automotive datasets but primarily evaluated on KITTI and Virtual KITTI 2 [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is efficient due to limited sampling: $N_s=6$ planes for background and $N_d=7$ points per dynamic object intersection [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of objects and the size of the MLPs. Latent codes are stored per object [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Optimization takes approximately one full day (350k iterations) on a single NVIDIA TITAN Xp GPU [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes with temporal consistency; separates static and dynamic elements; shares weights by object class; handles novel view synthesis well [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires 3D tracking data for object poses and bounding boxes; not limited to automotive datasets but primarily evaluated on KITTI/VKITTI [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Dynamic scene reconstruction and novel view synthesis where objects move rigidly and can be tracked [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Neural Scene Graphs were introduced in the paper "Neural Scene Graphs for Dynamic Scenes" [S1]. The method builds on neural radiance fields and sparse plane representations, extending them to handle dynamic scenes by decomposing them into static and dynamic components [S1]. It is used on automotive datasets like KITTI and Virtual KITTI 2 for dynamic scene reconstruction and novel view synthesis [S1].
+Neural Scene Graphs were introduced in 2021 by the authors of the paper "Neural Scene Graphs for Dynamic Scenes" [S1]. The method was developed to address limitations in existing neural rendering techniques (like NeRF) when applied to dynamic scenes, specifically ghosting and lack of temporal consistency. It builds upon the concept of Neural Graph Mapping by defining a specific DAG structure for dynamic environments [S1, Parent]. It has been used on automotive datasets like KITTI and Virtual KITTI 2 [S1].
 
